\paragraph{Training and the fixed-feature check.}
We use exact population expectations and bootstrapped target networks, with ten training runs per setting and feature dimensions $d=2,\ldots,11$.
For the check of \cref{lem:linear-action-difference}, we freeze the trained backward features and refit the two bottleneck forward vectors to the exact successor densities by $\rho$-weighted least squares.
We evaluate the fitted action-value difference using \eqref{eq:sm-value}.

\paragraph{Objectives of the ablation arms.}
Let $M$ be the exact successor matrix (one row per state-action pair, mass $1/(1-\gamma)$), $\bar M=(1-\gamma)M$ its normalized rows, $\rho=1/n$, and $X=nM$ the density ratios FB fits.
All rows carry equal weight in every objective.
FB fits $F_iB^\top$ to $X_i$ by squared error; its population optimum is the rank-$d$ truncated singular value decomposition of $X$, and the value is read as $F_i^\top z$ with $z=\rho\,B^\top r$.
\emph{FB, normalized target} fits $F_iB^\top$ to $\bar M_i$ with the same squared error and readout rescaled by $n/(1-\gamma)$, so its optimum coincides with FB's.
\emph{Softmax + squared error} predicts $p_i=\operatorname{softmax}(F_iB^\top)$ and minimizes $\tfrac12\sum_j(p_{ij}-\bar M_{ij})^2$; \name{} minimizes the cross-entropy $-\sum_j\bar M_{ij}\log p_{ij}$; both read the value as $\sum_j p_{ij}r_j/(1-\gamma)$.
\emph{\name{}, linear readout} keeps \name{}'s trained $F$ and $B$ but reads $F_i^\top z$ as FB does.
\emph{\name{}, soft immediate target} replaces the one-hot immediate term by a kernel over graph distance, $\exp(-\mathrm{dist}^2/(2\cdot0.5^2))$ row-normalized, so its fixed point is $\bar M$ smoothed by that kernel.
TD-trained arms use the bootstrapped targets of these objectives, $(1-\gamma)P_i+\gamma\,P_i\,\bar p^{\,\pi}_{\mathrm{target}}$ for the softmax arms and the corresponding squared TD error for the linear arms (with FB's orthogonality penalty at coefficient $1$), a target network with rate $0.005$, Adam, $5\times10^4$ steps, five seeds and two learning rates ($10^{-3}$, $10^{-2}$), reported as the mean over the ten runs.
The policy draws also change how long the two futures stay apart before they merge: on the three settings whose policies keep the process longer inside the clusters (R1, R3, R4), the difference between the futures carries $40$--$68\%$ of its squared mass inside the top three singular directions of $X$, against none on the other three, which is why FB recovers part of the difference at rank $3$ on those settings only.

\paragraph{Results across policies.}
\label{app:bottleneck-family-result}
\Cref{tab:family-rank} summarizes the feature dimension needed to reach $90\%$ of the true action-value difference in each setting.
Both softmax variants reach this threshold with two or three features, while FB requires five to ten.

